  m_text: ' stalagmites, flowstone, and drapery formations decorate many cave interiors.
    They grow exceptionally slowly, sometimes only a few centimeters every hundred
    years. Because of this, visitors are encouraged not to touch formations to protect
    their delicate surfaces. These ancient structures help tell the geological story
    of the region.


    Caves in Tennessee have also played important roles in human
    history. Indigenous peoples used caves for shelter, ceremonies, and art long
    before European settlers arrived. During the Civil War, some caves were mined
    for saltpeter, an important ingredient in gunpowder. Archaeologists have discovered
    tools, pottery, and bones that reveal how people interacted with these underground
    spaces. The cultural history of Tennessee caves adds depth to their scientific
    importance.


    Blue Spring Cave, the longest cave system in Tennessee, stretches
    more than 40 miles. This '
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
